  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "LIMITED",
    "confidence": 88,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports strong stability at the semantic-class level under nominally identical repetition: across 16 anchors repeated five times, no semantic instability was observed. Observable full-response state can nevertheless vary within the same semantic class, although some components—especially claim state, abstention, whole referent, and exact evidence sets—have limited measurement reliability and should be interpreted cautiously. Prompt paraphrasing and prior conversational history both alter response state relative to exact repetition, chiefly affecting claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior; neither condition produced semantic-class flips in the available data. Thus, explanations positing broad semantic randomness under identical questioning should be rejected for this dataset. Explanations based on sensitivity to wording and conversational context should be retained, but only as accounts of within-semantic response-state changes rather than meaning-level instability. The evidence is limited by the small anchor set and weaker reliability of several fine-grained measures."
